1
00:00:24,112 --> 00:00:30,040
Hello and welcome to the NPTEL course on Foundations for Virtual and Augmented Reality Systems.

2
00:00:31,902 --> 00:00:36,092
We are going to start our second lecture, lecture number 2,

3
00:00:36,092 --> 00:00:41,500
where we will cover basically some application areas and categories of systems

4
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:50,200
that we tend to see under virtual and augmented reality technologies.

5
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:57,830
Before I start, let me just quickly recollect what we learned in the previous lecture.

6
00:00:58,390 --> 00:01:01,193
In the previous lecture, we broadly introduced the field.

7
00:01:01,934 --> 00:01:05,638
Of course, many things were introduced at a very superficial level.

8
00:01:06,419 --> 00:01:12,185
As we progress in the course, we will slowly get into the details of those concepts.

9
00:01:12,685 --> 00:01:19,212
However, we got a broad overview of the field and also we got some introduction to the importance

10
00:01:19,613 --> 00:01:24,219
of the area, why we should know it, what is the potential, and so on.

11
00:01:25,701 --> 00:01:35,690
Now, in this lecture, we are going to cover different application areas pertaining to extended reality,

12
00:01:35,708 --> 00:01:39,019
primarily virtual reality and augmented reality.

13
00:01:39,039 --> 00:01:45,047
And also, while discussing the application areas, we will also quickly have a look at

14
00:01:45,297 --> 00:01:50,642
the different categories of systems from the application point of view that are there in the market.

15
00:01:51,024 --> 00:01:57,306
Now, if you recollect from our previous lecture,

16
00:01:57,430 --> 00:02:03,600
we talked about some use forecasts, what is likely to be the scenario down the line

17
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:08,100
in terms of application of this emerging technology that is extended reality.

18
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:13,903
Of course, it covers both augmented reality and virtual reality.

19
00:02:14,660 --> 00:02:20,026
As we have seen, there is a huge potential in different application areas.

20
00:02:20,206 --> 00:02:27,600
Say for example, if we see the previous, previously what we have discussed,

21
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:33,900
see under video games, we have mentioned that the use forecast is that about 34 percent

22
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:41,049
or more than one third of all the applications will include in one form or another the extended reality technologies.

23
00:02:42,671 --> 00:02:43,131
Then we have

24
00:02:44,225 --> 00:02:54,895
Healthcare, which again is going to have a significant component of these technologies as applications.

25
00:02:54,915 --> 00:03:02,843
Engineering, live events, video entertainment, real estate, retail, and military.

26
00:03:03,183 --> 00:03:06,286
Now, sometimes these statistics can be misleading.

27
00:03:06,306 --> 00:03:13,793
Say, for example, under military, it is mentioned that 4% of the application areas are likely to include AR and VR technologies.

28
00:03:14,161 --> 00:03:17,045
which, of course, may change as things progress.

29
00:03:17,105 --> 00:03:22,133
But even if we consider 4%, that is a huge sum of money

30
00:03:22,133 --> 00:03:32,964
because military budgets worldwide are huge and this 4% translates to a significant amount of money.

31
00:03:35,527 --> 00:03:40,894
So, both percentage-wise as well as value-wise, we can expect

32
00:03:41,465 --> 00:03:46,751
good penetration of these technologies in the commercial space.

33
00:03:47,833 --> 00:03:52,979
We have already discussed different market forecasts and other things in the previous lecture.

34
00:03:54,461 --> 00:04:08,678
Now, let us quickly have a look at the different applications that are currently there, which are coming up in different domains related to virtual reality.

35
00:04:09,238 --> 00:04:10,780
Then we will talk about augmented reality.

36
00:04:13,038 --> 00:04:22,011
Now, again, if we recollect from the previous lecture, you can notice that we have used the term immersive technologies.

37
00:04:22,031 --> 00:04:30,563
So, VR technologies essentially refer to immersive technologies where our senses are blocked and we are transported to the virtual world.

38
00:04:31,965 --> 00:04:41,900
Now, there are several application areas pertaining to immersive technologies, several application domains are there and still it is evolving,

39
00:04:41,900 --> 00:04:44,715
So, newer domains may add.

40
00:04:45,296 --> 00:04:51,264
However, whatever is currently there, let us have a quick look.

41
00:04:51,284 --> 00:04:53,867
The first thing is gaming and entertainment.

42
00:04:54,448 --> 00:04:57,172
This is where everything started, actually.

43
00:04:57,752 --> 00:05:01,057
As you can see, gaming and entertainment, of course, is a very old area.

44
00:05:01,077 --> 00:05:12,913
However, the recent trend is that more and more gaming solutions and gaming products that are coming into the market rely on immersive technologies.

45
00:05:13,265 --> 00:05:19,117
So that it becomes more acceptable to the users and more widely popular.

46
00:05:23,346 --> 00:05:31,643
So, this immersive gaming experience is a relatively new phenomenon, which is adding value to the gaming industry.

47
00:05:32,564 --> 00:05:34,901
And in this application,

48
00:05:34,901 --> 00:05:45,610
the gamer is expected to wear headsets or other VR devices to experience immersion while playing the game.

49
00:05:45,610 --> 00:05:53,801
Another prominent application domain for VR is healthcare.

50
00:05:56,765 --> 00:06:02,492
The applications primarily cater to medical education and training.

51
00:06:04,531 --> 00:06:10,319
Now, here I would like to mention that not only VR, but MR is also used.

52
00:06:10,739 --> 00:06:11,280
What is MR?

53
00:06:11,300 --> 00:06:13,884
We will see in a later discussion.

54
00:06:16,487 --> 00:06:26,276
So, for example, here in this image as you can see the doctors can, potential doctors can learn about

55
00:06:26,276 --> 00:06:34,251
different body organs, how they work, how they look, what are the different parts in the organs while going through medical training.

56
00:06:34,889 --> 00:06:53,732
Sometimes it is essential because there is a real problem of getting cadavers, and in such scenarios these simulations and immersive training modules can help a lot.

57
00:06:59,059 --> 00:07:02,483
Education is another emerging domain where

58
00:07:03,121 --> 00:07:10,591
immersive technologies have found lots of applications starting from the primary level to the tertiary level.

59
00:07:11,993 --> 00:07:31,558
So, it has been found that with an appropriate amount of exposure to immersive technologies can lead to improving learners' engagement and learning outcomes vis-a-vis the standard

60
00:07:31,775 --> 00:07:35,562
training, teaching, and learning methods.

61
00:07:35,582 --> 00:07:45,641
But of course, we have to be careful while exposing a potential learner to the immersive environment; too long duration may create problems as we shall see later.

62
00:07:48,586 --> 00:07:55,489
As the image shows, this type of application caters to both primary education as well as tertiary education

63
00:07:55,489 --> 00:08:01,897
Classroom education is one prime example for primary level or secondary level education, whereas,

64
00:08:01,897 --> 00:08:14,556
higher educational institutes also come up with their own immersive educational solutions for different topic-wise teaching and learning.

65
00:08:20,847 --> 00:08:28,015
Although it is yet to gain too much popularity, it is an evolving area, that is the automobile industry.

66
00:08:28,535 --> 00:08:41,344
So, typically what happens is that to increase customer satisfaction and customer experience, many companies are nowadays coming up with immersive experiences of

67
00:08:41,344 --> 00:08:49,437
using a newly launched car model without visiting, say for example, the showroom,

68
00:08:49,889 --> 00:08:51,310
or actually driving a car.

69
00:08:54,113 --> 00:09:09,407
Also, although it is primarily meant for potential buyers, it can also have other applications pertaining to the automobile industry including the design and development stage.

70
00:09:16,013 --> 00:09:19,356
Travel and tourism is also coming up heavily.

71
00:09:19,758 --> 00:09:23,543
which includes these immersive technologies.

72
00:09:26,187 --> 00:09:27,929
The objectives are manifold.

73
00:09:29,151 --> 00:09:39,600
Say for example, if I want to give the experience of visiting a place to a user who cannot for some reason visit that place,

74
00:09:39,600 --> 00:09:46,795
that is called virtual tours, and virtual tourism has become quite popular nowadays.

75
00:09:49,508 --> 00:09:56,115
because it saves money, saves physical effort, while letting the user explore faraway places.

76
00:09:57,297 --> 00:10:04,705
Another interesting thing that is coming up nowadays is the preservation of digital heritage and heritage tourism.

77
00:10:05,186 --> 00:10:17,760
So, for certain reasons, it is not possible to let a large number of users visit a heritage place, but

78
00:10:18,466 --> 00:10:24,333
or all parts of a heritage place from the point of view of preserving the heritage.

79
00:10:25,394 --> 00:10:37,088
But we can let the users visit the heritage place virtually, thereby enabling the user to explore each and every part of the heritage space without physically going there.

80
00:10:38,049 --> 00:10:42,735
Now that is one important application area that is evolving.

81
00:10:43,542 --> 00:10:50,312
heritage tourism and this also leads to preservation of the heritage space.

82
00:10:50,833 --> 00:10:59,125
So, there can be physical decay, but we can preserve the space digitally in the form of heritage virtual tours,

83
00:10:59,125 --> 00:11:10,123
which can increase the lifespan and which can increase the potential of the space to be visited by many more users.

84
00:11:16,313 --> 00:11:27,969
Later on, we will talk about one case study where we will see how this digital heritage preservation is getting benefited by the use of these immersive technologies.

85
00:11:31,073 --> 00:11:34,037
Real estate and architecture, another prominent application area.

86
00:11:35,018 --> 00:11:46,253
Now here, like automobiles, a potential buyer of a, say, upcoming real estate

87
00:11:46,958 --> 00:11:57,194
or upcoming property can explore the property without actually visiting the place, or it may so happen that the place does not even exist.

88
00:11:57,655 --> 00:12:06,869
It is yet to be made, but beforehand the user can experience and make an informed choice about whether to go for buying it or not.

89
00:12:11,716 --> 00:12:14,080
So, that is of course,

90
00:12:14,836 --> 00:12:25,693
of great benefit to potential buyers who wish to spend a lot of money on real estate.

91
00:12:25,713 --> 00:12:29,619
Last but not least is defense applications.

92
00:12:30,059 --> 00:12:37,671
As I said, there is scope for several defense-related applications of immersive technology.

93
00:12:39,514 --> 00:12:42,919
Now there can be several use cases of application of such technologies.

94
00:12:43,473 --> 00:12:45,997
starting from specialized military training.

95
00:12:47,399 --> 00:13:05,245
So, here the actual vehicles, soldiers, and combat environment can be simulated in a virtual world with constraints put into place so that somebody can get trained on real-world scenarios.

96
00:13:06,647 --> 00:13:12,315
Then this virtual boot camp and combat training can be another application area.

97
00:13:12,700 --> 00:13:26,498
where HMDs with motion trackers, full-body tracking systems, and wireless weapon controllers as per the user can be used for training.

98
00:13:29,341 --> 00:13:37,912
As we have already discussed, in medical education, immersive technologies play a crucial role in training.

99
00:13:38,874 --> 00:13:42,218
The same thing can be applied for military-related medical personnel.

100
00:13:43,345 --> 00:13:45,868
who wish to get trained on different scenarios.

101
00:13:46,509 --> 00:13:49,253
So, we can have immersive technology applications for that.

102
00:13:50,855 --> 00:14:04,152
Even maintenance and assembly technician training can also be considered for such personnel in the military establishment.

103
00:14:05,774 --> 00:14:07,176
So, different forms of training

104
00:14:09,248 --> 00:14:14,815
including combat training, medical training, assembly line training, and so on, can be thought of.

105
00:14:16,657 --> 00:14:19,441
And there are already applications catering to these needs.

106
00:14:21,864 --> 00:14:32,777
So, that is broadly what the major application areas of virtual reality technologies are.

107
00:14:32,797 --> 00:14:35,020
Now, there are many systems

108
00:14:35,303 --> 00:14:39,350
in those application areas and some other not-so-prominent application areas.

109
00:14:39,870 --> 00:14:44,218
Now, let us have a quick look at how these systems can be categorized.

110
00:14:44,638 --> 00:14:47,443
What are the major categories of systems?

111
00:14:47,463 --> 00:14:52,992
This is from a systems point of view or systemic view of this field.

112
00:14:57,680 --> 00:15:06,030
So, we can roughly link it to the user experiences, what kind of experiences the user is going to get using those systems.

113
00:15:08,874 --> 00:15:18,405
Although at the beginning I mentioned that VR refers to immersive technology, the applications that are already developed that are in the market,

114
00:15:20,647 --> 00:15:24,351
they actually offer varying degrees of immersion.

115
00:15:24,692 --> 00:15:28,817
It is not that every application is fully immersive.

116
00:15:28,837 --> 00:15:39,610
So, varying degrees of immersion can be expected from the applications that are already in the market, and depending on that degree of immersion we can categorize the VR systems.

117
00:15:41,952 --> 00:15:47,599
Now, as I said, depending on immersion,

118
00:15:48,001 --> 00:15:49,062
there can be categories.

119
00:15:49,102 --> 00:15:51,065
So, broadly there are three categories.

120
00:15:52,446 --> 00:15:53,768
First one is non-immersive.

121
00:15:53,788 --> 00:16:04,600
This may sound counterintuitive because we are talking about immersive technologies whereas we are starting our system description with non-immersive.

122
00:16:04,620 --> 00:16:11,788
But why we can think of it as virtual reality from the system's point of view, we will discuss.

123
00:16:13,691 --> 00:16:15,713
The second category is semi-immersive.

124
00:16:16,790 --> 00:16:20,816
And the third category is fully immersive.

125
00:16:20,836 --> 00:16:25,323
Now, let us quickly have a look at each of these categories and what they actually refer to.

126
00:16:27,846 --> 00:16:31,171
Non-immersive, as I said, seems to be counterintuitive.

127
00:16:31,191 --> 00:16:34,837
Why should it be part of virtual reality where we are talking about immersion?

128
00:16:35,838 --> 00:16:45,312
However, when we are referring to non-immersive VR systems, what we are referring to is virtual experience

129
00:16:47,722 --> 00:16:58,654
obtained through a computer where the user can control some characters or activities within the software but the environment is not directly interacting with the user.

130
00:17:03,059 --> 00:17:15,653
Now this definition, of course, is a bit cryptic, a bit complex, and we must admit that this is often overlooked as VR because already

131
00:17:16,223 --> 00:17:17,925
These types of systems are very common.

132
00:17:17,944 --> 00:17:22,872
However, not all computer applications are considered to be non-immersive VR.

133
00:17:23,252 --> 00:17:25,555
We will be considering specific applications.

134
00:17:25,595 --> 00:17:29,421
Which kind of applications?

135
00:17:29,441 --> 00:17:35,609
That provide computer-generated environments but allow users to keep control of their physical environment.

136
00:17:35,629 --> 00:17:40,375
Now, these types of systems,

137
00:17:40,945 --> 00:17:51,443
They rely on computer or video game console displays, console displays, and input devices like keyboards and mice and also handheld controllers.

138
00:17:51,924 --> 00:17:56,773
Now, this seems to be our everyday use of computer systems.

139
00:17:57,394 --> 00:17:58,696
So, what is so special?

140
00:17:58,736 --> 00:18:00,179
Why are we calling it VR?

141
00:18:01,441 --> 00:18:04,827
Before that, let us just quickly have a look at what kind of systems we are referring to.

142
00:18:05,701 --> 00:18:21,238
These are essentially gaming devices with the gaming experience such as PlayStation, Xbox, etc., which we are calling non-immersive VR.

143
00:18:23,260 --> 00:18:30,088
When we are playing a game with such systems such as World of Warcraft or similar games,

144
00:18:31,351 --> 00:18:34,455
we are calling it non-immersive.

145
00:18:34,475 --> 00:18:38,099
Note that we are specifically referring to games.

146
00:18:38,700 --> 00:18:40,703
We are not saying any applications.

147
00:18:41,704 --> 00:18:42,605
Now why is that so?

148
00:18:43,346 --> 00:18:49,073
There can be other applications also which can be considered to be non-immersive if they satisfy the following.

149
00:18:50,555 --> 00:18:56,442
Now when I say immersion, essentially the idea is that our senses are blocked.

150
00:18:56,522 --> 00:19:00,447
So we are unable to receive any input from our real world environment.

151
00:19:02,317 --> 00:19:03,639
And that is what immersion is.

152
00:19:03,679 --> 00:19:05,962
We are only getting input from the virtual world.

153
00:19:06,663 --> 00:19:22,384
Now, when we are actually playing a very engaging video game with video game consoles and accessories and input devices, many times it may be noticed that the user is fully

154
00:19:22,786 --> 00:19:29,973
quote unquote immersed in that game that means if you are saying something or something is happening outside the user is not aware of it

155
00:19:29,973 --> 00:19:42,158
although the eyes ears and other sensory organs are open but because the mind is completely in the game so they are oblivious to the real world surrounding

156
00:19:42,533 --> 00:19:47,916
whatever is happening around them only if we are having such applications

157
00:19:48,908 --> 00:19:50,550
then we can call it non-immersive.

158
00:19:50,570 --> 00:19:57,881
So, it is kind of psychological engagement, although physically the user is attached to the real world.

159
00:19:58,742 --> 00:20:00,184
Gaming is a prime example.

160
00:20:00,384 --> 00:20:10,354
However, there may be other applications you can think of, which completely engage the user, completely immersing the user in the particular digital environment,

161
00:20:10,354 --> 00:20:15,325
so that although other senses are open, they are still unable to experience it.

162
00:20:16,006 --> 00:20:18,730
That type of application we are calling non-immersive.

163
00:20:19,958 --> 00:20:21,841
This is purely a systemic point of view.

164
00:20:25,005 --> 00:20:27,890
Although psychologically that is the same as immersion.

165
00:20:27,910 --> 00:20:31,435
Now the next category of system is semi-immersive.

166
00:20:31,455 --> 00:20:32,336
What is semi-immersive?

167
00:20:32,456 --> 00:20:38,425
A mixture of non-immersive and fully immersive VR.

168
00:20:38,445 --> 00:20:39,947
Ideally, we should not call it a mixture.

169
00:20:39,967 --> 00:20:43,372
We call it in between non-immersive and fully immersive VR.

170
00:20:45,275 --> 00:20:47,839
Where user can have partial,

171
00:20:49,051 --> 00:20:58,426
Immersive experience by blocking some of the sensory organs, not all.

172
00:20:58,466 --> 00:21:11,447
Say if we are blocking only eyes, but other senses like our ears, our skin, they are exposed to real world signals, then we can call it partial immersion because only eyes are blocked.

173
00:21:12,469 --> 00:21:16,155
I cannot see anything; we cannot see anything from the real world.

174
00:21:19,055 --> 00:21:27,125
So, when we are using partially immersive systems, that means some of our senses are free to sense the real world around us.

175
00:21:33,733 --> 00:21:46,328
Now, it can be in the form of a 3D space or virtual environment where users can move on his or her own either through a computer screen or a VR box or headset, typically VR box.

176
00:21:46,561 --> 00:21:53,531
A VR headset is the common example of a semi-immersive experience.

177
00:21:56,735 --> 00:22:01,302
And in such cases, users typically do not have any real physical movements.

178
00:22:02,764 --> 00:22:09,413
In most cases, in 90% of cases, you can say, other than the visual experience.

179
00:22:12,177 --> 00:22:18,308
Semi-immersive offers the most cost-effective and commonly used among all forms of VR systems.

180
00:22:21,413 --> 00:22:26,763
Virtual tours experienced with only wearing a headset can be an example of a semi-immersive system.

181
00:22:28,866 --> 00:22:36,700
And this type of system can be both device-based as well as web-based, can be experienced by connecting to the web.

182
00:22:43,514 --> 00:22:50,242
Now, fully immersive, as opposed to non-immersive or semi-immersive, is a completely different class altogether.

183
00:22:50,963 --> 00:22:54,367
Now, this is actually what immersion means.

184
00:22:54,387 --> 00:23:01,576
That is, we are completely blocking the user's ability to sense the real world by blocking all the senses by some means.

185
00:23:03,218 --> 00:23:08,965
And the user is only sensing the things that are happening in the virtual world.

186
00:23:10,784 --> 00:23:21,256
So, these types of systems are supposed to give a sense of being present fully in that virtual world and that everything is happening to the user for real.

187
00:23:21,696 --> 00:23:26,362
So, the user's sense of reality is replaced by virtual realism.

188
00:23:33,430 --> 00:23:38,175
As you can guess, these fully immersive systems are the

189
00:23:38,459 --> 00:23:41,664
costliest VR systems that we can have.

190
00:23:43,127 --> 00:23:47,073
They involve lots of devices to fully block our senses.

191
00:23:47,093 --> 00:23:58,071
For example, helmets, gloves, body connectors with sense detectors, and body suits essentially.

192
00:23:58,793 --> 00:24:08,790
With those things on the body, users' movements, reactions, and even a blink of an eye can be detected and accordingly translated to actions in the virtual world.

193
00:24:11,896 --> 00:24:18,587
So, all these things are there to let the user feel or perceive that they are in the virtual world physically.

194
00:24:19,709 --> 00:24:22,033
So, they always perceive that they are

195
00:24:22,975 --> 00:24:27,221
in reality, whereas they are actually cut off from reality and in the virtual world.

196
00:24:27,561 --> 00:24:32,789
So, if we are able to give the user that sense, then that type of system is called fully immersive.

197
00:24:35,513 --> 00:24:41,421
There are many examples we can have for virtual shooting game zones.

198
00:24:42,663 --> 00:24:51,615
In that zone, users will be wearing all these things; that is, a dedicated space where users will be wearing all these accessories.

199
00:24:52,220 --> 00:24:55,545
to experience a fully immersive virtual world.

200
00:24:57,387 --> 00:25:01,313
All users' actions will be translated to actions in the virtual world.

201
00:25:01,473 --> 00:25:03,736
Users will not be able to sense anything in the real world.

202
00:25:05,238 --> 00:25:08,983
So different applications are there for these types of things.

203
00:25:10,886 --> 00:25:20,860
Say for example, users make actual movements; users make actual movements, move arms and body to run, jump, crouch, shoot, throw,

204
00:25:22,122 --> 00:25:28,433
and many more such things in the virtual world.

205
00:25:28,453 --> 00:25:33,729
User is viewing the environment through a helmet or head mounted display

206
00:25:33,729 --> 00:25:45,042
and user is equipped with other gears to sense body movements and translate it to the things that are happening in the virtual world.

207
00:25:47,857 --> 00:25:52,062
Now, among the three categories of systems, fully immersive is the costliest one.

208
00:25:52,763 --> 00:25:57,108
Commercially, not many applications are available because it is difficult to afford.

209
00:25:57,849 --> 00:26:01,614
You will not find them in many places.

210
00:26:02,735 --> 00:26:08,923
More common are the semi-immersive and non-immersive systems.

211
00:26:09,123 --> 00:26:17,593
However, non-immersive, as I said, are a special category of regular computer systems, software, which

212
00:26:17,978 --> 00:26:22,325
completely takes away your attention, completely immerses you in the interaction.

213
00:26:23,246 --> 00:26:26,972
Those are limited in set, limited in nature.

214
00:26:29,636 --> 00:26:35,606
More commonly, we can say that these are the semi-immersive ones.

215
00:26:35,626 --> 00:26:40,253
Now, semi-immersive ones give the feeling of immersion partially.

216
00:26:40,874 --> 00:26:43,598
Of course, the user is still free to sense the real world.

217
00:26:44,725 --> 00:26:52,834
So most of the commercial immersive applications that you may come across are actually semi-immersive because they do not block all the sensors.

218
00:26:56,519 --> 00:27:04,027
So with that, we come to the end of this discussion on VR applications and system categories.

219
00:27:06,651 --> 00:27:09,274
What we have learned are the different application domains.

220
00:27:09,314 --> 00:27:11,116
We have covered a few important domains.

221
00:27:11,216 --> 00:27:13,939
There may be other domains which are still evolving.

222
00:27:14,392 --> 00:27:16,194
but are yet to become very prominent.

223
00:27:16,695 --> 00:27:28,409
But as the use forecast says, VR is going to be a very important technology in multiple domains and a lot of effort will be given to develop such applications.

224
00:27:28,429 --> 00:27:33,115
The second thing is we talked about categories of VR applications.

225
00:27:33,135 --> 00:27:36,939
We mentioned three categories: non-immersive, semi-immersive, and fully immersive.

226
00:27:38,501 --> 00:27:39,663
Now, that is about VR.

227
00:27:40,243 --> 00:27:44,128
So, in the next part of this lecture, we will be covering

228
00:27:44,463 --> 00:27:47,451
AR applications.

229
00:27:47,471 --> 00:27:50,299
So, with that, we come to the end of this part of the lecture.

230
00:27:50,960 --> 00:27:55,192
Hope you have understood the concepts and enjoyed the discussion.

231
00:27:56,716 --> 00:27:57,157
See you again.

232
00:27:57,839 --> 00:27:59,383
Happy learning, and thank you.
